%S A400920 3,5565047531,11275486097,11925875327,34085972027,44232022697,
%T A400920 48246520337,80347204211,97472326421,102964188797,103202776097,
%U A400920 105098734127,119725258031,125279537861,127813460111,138995281427,149721804611,153806779337,164675373647,168210997607,245783409167
%N A400920 Primes p such that p+2, (p-1)^2+1, (p-1)^2+3, (p-1)^4+1, (p-1)^4+3 and (p-1)^8+1 are all prime.
%C A400920 Equivalently, with q = p-1, the seven numbers q+1, q+3, q^2+1, q^2+3, q^4+1, q^4+3 and q^8+1 are all prime.
%C A400920 The numbers (p-1)^2+1, (p-1)^4+1 and (p-1)^8+1 are iterates of T(n) = (n-1)^2+1 starting at p.
%C A400920 As in supersequence A400684, p = 3 (q = 2) is included.
%C A400920 For every term p > 3, q = p-1 == 4 (mod 6). Proof: p and p+2 are both prime and > 3, so p is odd and p == 2 (mod 3), since p == 1 (mod 3) would give 3 | p+2. Hence q is even and q == 1 (mod 3), so q == 4 (mod 6).
%C A400920 For every term p > 3, q = p-1 must lie in one of the residue classes 1156, 1666, 1786, 1870, 2296, 2380, 2500, 3010 (mod 3570), the classes for which none of the seven numbers is divisible by 2, 3, 5, 7 or 17 (finite check).
%C A400920 The Bateman-Horn conjecture predicts that this sequence is infinite. This is a conjecture, not proved.
%e A400920 p = 3 (q = p-1 = 2) is a term since p = 3, p+2 = 5, q^2+1 = 5, q^2+3 = 7, q^4+1 = 17, q^4+3 = 19 and q^8+1 = 257 are all prime.
%e A400920 p = 5565047531 (q = 5565047530) is a term since p = 5565047531, p+2 = 5565047533, q^2+1 = 30969754011159100901, q^2+3 = 30969754011159100903, q^4+1 = 959125663511705219592882732896380810001, q^4+3 = 959125663511705219592882732896380810003 and q^8+1 = 919922038406768785267010714299586648091086088343576011893844396536256100000001 are all prime.
%o A400920 (Python)
%o A400920 from sympy import isprime
%o A400920 def ok(p):
%o A400920     q = p - 1
%o A400920     return all(isprime(t) for t in [p, p+2, q**2+1, q**2+3, q**4+1, q**4+3, q**8+1])
